    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d24">

                <pb facs="0070" n="16" xml:id="pg16"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to the President’s Special Assistant</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d24-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-08-10">10 August 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Prime Minister to Mr. Hopkins Top Secret and Personal.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>I was on the point of telegraphing to the President about stage II<anchor n="2" xml:id="d24-f2"/>
                        when I heard of your conversations with Campbell and Brand.<anchor n="3"
                            xml:id="d24-f3"/></item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>I agree with you about the necessity of obtaining a directive from the
                        President about stage II, which is a war
                        period.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Much now turns on our meeting. The President has asked for a week more
                        before giving a date and a rendezvous.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d24-f4"/> He
                        seems to be cooling off Scotland and talks of Bermuda. I am trying to
                        persuade him to come to <hi
                                rendition="#small-caps">Quadrant</hi>. The worst accounts
                        are given of the Bermuda climate in September. Let us know what you advise
                        at earliest. I was counting of course on your coming with him.<anchor n="5"
                            xml:id="d24-f5"/></item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>It was my idea to discuss the question of a directive with him at this
                        meeting.</item>
                    <label>5.</label>
                    <item>Meanwhile I hope that he will be able to issue directions to all concerned
                        so as to prevent decisions being taken regarding American programmes for
                        munitions and other supplies.</item>
                    <label>6.</label>
                    <item>Perhaps you will let me know in your reply whether in the light of the
                        above you still think that you should have at once a detailed statement of
                        our case. If so it will of course be furnished, but we are inclined to
                        think, subject to your views, that it may be better to discuss it with you
                        at the meeting.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d24-f6"/></item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d24-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d24-f1-content">
                <p>Sent by the United States Military Attaché, London, via Army channels.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d24-f2" n="2" xml:id="d24-f2-content">
                <p>The phrases “stage II” and “phase II” refer to the furnishing of lend-lease
                    aid in the period between the surrender of Germany and the surrender of Japan.
                    For background on Anglo-American discussions on this subject, see <ref
                        target="frus1944v03#pg_31"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1944, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, pp. 31</ref> ff.; Hancock
                    and Gowing, pp. 515 ff.; Hall, pp. 434–447.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d24-f3" n="3" xml:id="d24-f3-content">
                <p>No memoranda have been found of Hopkins–Campbell–Brand conversations on this
                    subject.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d24-f4" n="4" xml:id="d24-f4-content">
                <p>See <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg13">p. 13</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d24-f5" n="5" xml:id="d24-f5-content">
                <p>In a telegram of July 20, 1944 (not printed), Hopkins had told Churchill that he
                    hoped to be present at the proposed conference between Roosevelt and Churchill
                    (Hopkins Papers).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d24-f6" n="6" xml:id="d24-f6-content">
                <p>Although Hopkins’ reply to Churchill (<hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg17">p. 17</ref>) did not request the detailed statement referred
                    to, Brand prepared a memorandum on the British position on August 14, 1944, and
                    gave it to Hopkins. See <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg159"
                        >p. 159</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
